Beat 2 — enable the trigram extension                                                                                                        
                                                                                                                                               
  Why an extension at all? PostgreSQL ships with a small core and a pile of optional features bundled but not loaded by default — trigram, GIS,
   UUID generators, and others. You opt in per-database with one SQL command: CREATE EXTENSION pg_trgm;. Django wraps that command in a
  migration operation called TrigramExtension() so it goes through your normal migrate workflow.                                               
                  
  Why a migration and not a one-off psql command? Migrations are reproducible. Today you enable it on your dev DB; six months from now when you
   set up a staging/prod DB, running migrate on the fresh database enables it again automatically. No "wait, did we run that command?" mystery.

  ---
  Three steps. All in your terminal.

  1. Generate an empty migration scaffold:

  python manage.py makemigrations posts --empty --name enable_trigram_extension

  This creates a new file under posts/migrations/ with a number prefix (probably 0002_enable_trigram_extension.py). It's empty — Django
  scaffolded the structure but didn't put any operations in (because nothing in your models changed).

  2. Open that file and replace its contents with this:

  from django.db import migrations
  from django.contrib.postgres.operations import TrigramExtension


  class Migration(migrations.Migration):

      dependencies = [
          ('posts', '0001_initial'),
      ]

      operations = [
          TrigramExtension(),
      ]

  Two things to notice:
  - dependencies — the migration system's "this migration must run AFTER that one" graph. Django pre-filled this; just leave it.
  - TrigramExtension() — the only operation. Translates to CREATE EXTENSION IF NOT EXISTS pg_trgm; at migrate time.

  3. Apply it:

  python manage.py migrate

  You should see Django log:
  Applying posts.0002_enable_trigram_extension... OK

  ---
  Verify in pgAdmin (optional, but worth doing once to see what the extension actually IS):

  In a new query tab, against blog_db:
  SELECT * FROM pg_extension;

  You'll see a row for pg_trgm alongside the default plpgsql. That row is the extension being installed. From this moment on, blog_db knows
  what trigrams are.

  You can also do:
  SELECT similarity('dhurandhar', 'dhruandhar');

  Returns a number around 0.33. The same math from beat 1 — just done by PostgreSQL directly. This proves the extension is live before we even
  touch Django.

  ---
  Run those three steps and tell me what you see. Then beat 3 is using TrigramSimilarity from Django to score real posts.
