Beat 3 — TrigramSimilarity from Django                                                                                                       
                                                                                                                                             
  This is the moment that proves the typo bug is dead.                                                                                         
   
  Open the shell:                                                                                                                              
  python manage.py shell

  Test 1 — Dhurandhar typo case (the one we kept hitting):
  from django.contrib.postgres.search import TrigramSimilarity
  from posts.models import Post

  results = (
      Post.objects
      .annotate(similarity=TrigramSimilarity('title', 'dhurandhar'))
      .filter(similarity__gte=0.3)
      .order_by('-similarity')
  )
  for p in results:
      print(round(p.similarity, 3), p.title)

  Expected output (something like):
  1.0    Dhurandhar 2025 Blockbuster
  0.41   Dhruandhar in japan

  Both posts. The first is an exact match (similarity = 1.0). The second is the typo, scored ~0.4 — above your 0.3 threshold, so it's in.
  Compare to full-text search which only returned 1.

  Test 2 — Blockbuster typo case:
  results = (
      Post.objects
      .annotate(similarity=TrigramSimilarity('title', 'blockbuster'))
      .filter(similarity__gte=0.3)
      .order_by('-similarity')
  )
  for p in results:
      print(round(p.similarity, 3), p.title)

  Expected:
  0.6X   Dhurandhar 2025 Blockbuster
  0.4X   Raone india Blocbuster      ← the typo finally caught

  Test 3 — wildly wrong query, expect zero:
  results = (
      Post.objects
      .annotate(similarity=TrigramSimilarity('title', 'kubernetes'))
      .filter(similarity__gte=0.3)
      .order_by('-similarity')
  )
  for p in results:
      print(round(p.similarity, 3), p.title)

  Should print nothing. Confirms the threshold is doing its job — fuzzy doesn't mean "match anything."

  ---
  SQL parallel (drop into pgAdmin if you want to see it raw):

  SELECT title, similarity(title, 'dhurandhar') AS sim
  FROM posts_post
  WHERE similarity(title, 'dhurandhar') >= 0.3
  ORDER BY sim DESC;

  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────┐
  │                   ORM                    │               SQL               │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ TrigramSimilarity('title', 'dhurandhar') │ similarity(title, 'dhurandhar') │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ .annotate(similarity=...)                │ AS sim                          │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ .filter(similarity__gte=0.3)             │ WHERE similarity(...) >= 0.3    │
  ├──────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
  │ .order_by('-similarity')                 │ ORDER BY sim DESC               │
  └──────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────┘

  Same pattern as SearchRank — annotate a virtual column, filter on it, order by it.

  ----------------------------------------------------
Interesting result — only ONE post matched, not the two we expected. There's a real subtlety here worth catching.                            
                                                                                                                                               
  ---                                                                                                                                          
  What happened:                                                                                                                               
                                                                                                                                               
  similarity('Dhurandhar 2025 Blockbuster', 'dhurandhar') = 0.39                                                                               
                  
  Even the exact-match post barely scraped above the threshold. Why? Because similarity() compares whole strings against whole strings. Your
  title has extra words ("2025", "Blockbuster") that contribute trigrams the query doesn't have — and those extra trigrams dilute the score.

  The math for "Dhruandhar in japan" vs "dhurandhar":
  - title trigrams: ~22 (because "in japan" adds chunks too)
  - query trigrams: ~10
  - shared: ~4
  - score: 4 / (22 + 10 − 4) ≈ 0.14 → below 0.3 → filtered out

  So similarity() is the wrong tool when you're searching a short query inside a longer string. It's designed for full-string-to-full-string
  comparison (like name dedup, where both sides are about the same length).

  ---
  The right tool: TrigramWordSimilarity (Django) / word_similarity() (PostgreSQL).

  Instead of comparing whole strings, it finds the best-matching word inside the longer text and scores against that. Exactly what we want for
  search.

  Re-run test in the shell:

  from django.contrib.postgres.search import TrigramWordSimilarity
  from posts.models import Post

  results = (
      Post.objects
      .annotate(similarity=TrigramWordSimilarity('dhurandhar', 'title'))
      .filter(similarity__gte=0.3)
      .order_by('-similarity')
  )
  for p in results:
      print(round(p.similarity, 3), p.title)

  Note the argument order is reversed: TrigramWordSimilarity('search_term', 'field_name') — the search query comes FIRST, the field comes
  SECOND. This is the opposite of TrigramSimilarity. Easy to fumble; worth a mental sticky note.

  You should now see both posts:
  ~1.0    Dhurandhar 2025 Blockbuster
  ~0.7    Dhruandhar in japan

  SQL parallel for pgAdmin:
  SELECT title, word_similarity('dhurandhar', title) AS sim
  FROM posts_post
  WHERE word_similarity('dhurandhar', title) >= 0.3
  ORDER BY sim DESC;

  Note word_similarity('term', column) — same reversed order.

  ---
  Quick mental model — when to use which:

  ┌───────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
  │       Function        │                                   Use when                                    │
  ├───────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ TrigramSimilarity     │ Comparing two whole strings of similar length (name dedup, brand match)       │
  ├───────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ TrigramWordSimilarity │ Searching a short query inside a longer text (this is what real search needs) │
  └───────────────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘

  For our purposes — searching titles for a user-typed term — TrigramWordSimilarity is the right call.
