 Beat 4 — combine full-text + trigram with Q objects                                                                                          
                                                                                                                                               
  Why combine? Each catches what the other misses:                                                                                             
                  
  ┌────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬─────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────┐
  │                      Reader types                      │   Full-text catches?    │      Trigram catches?      │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤
  │ running (wants articles about runners)                 │ ✅ stems to run         │ ❌ very different from run │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤
  │ dhurandhar (correct spelling, but other post has typo) │ ⚠️  only the correct one │ ✅ both posts              │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤
  │ djurandhar (heavy typo)                                │ ❌ no match             │ ✅ still matches           │
  ├────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┤
  │ movies (wants singular too)                            │ ✅ stems to movi        │ ⚠️  only similar spellings  │
  └────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────┘

  Real readers paraphrase AND misspell. Production search uses both clauses, joined with OR — match if EITHER side says yes.

  ---
  The blocker: .filter() defaults to AND. When you write .filter(rank__gte=0.001, similarity__gte=0.3), Django translates to WHERE rank >=
  0.001 AND similarity >= 0.3. We need OR. Django's tool for that is Q objects.

  from django.db.models import Q

  # AND (default)
  .filter(rank__gte=0.001, similarity__gte=0.3)

  # OR — wrap each side in Q, join with |
  .filter(Q(rank__gte=0.001) | Q(similarity__gte=0.3))

  The operators:

  ┌─────────────┬─────────┐
  │  Q syntax   │   SQL   │
  ├─────────────┼─────────┤
  │ Q(a) | Q(b) │ a OR b  │
  ├─────────────┼─────────┤
  │ Q(a) & Q(b) │ a AND b │
  ├─────────────┼─────────┤
  │ ~Q(a)       │ NOT a   │
  └─────────────┴─────────┘

  That's it. Q is a wrapper that lets |, &, ~ mean OR / AND / NOT inside .filter(). Without Q, you can't express OR in a filter at all.

  ---
  Try the combined query in the shell:

  from django.db.models import Q
  from django.contrib.postgres.search import SearchQuery, SearchRank, TrigramWordSimilarity
  from posts.models import Post

  q = 'dhurandhar'
  search_query = SearchQuery(q)

  results = (
      Post.objects
      .annotate(
          rank=SearchRank('search_vector', search_query),
          similarity=TrigramWordSimilarity(q, 'title'),
      )
      .filter(Q(rank__gte=0.001) | Q(similarity__gte=0.3))
      .order_by('-rank', '-similarity')
  )

  for p in results:
      print(round(p.rank, 4), round(p.similarity, 3), p.title)

  Expect both Dhurandhar posts. Each row's two scores tell you which side caught it — high rank = caught by full-text, high similarity = caught
   by trigram.

  Then test a heavy typo to prove trigram is doing real work:
  q = 'djurandhar'   # nonsense word — won't be in the search_vector

  Re-run the same block. Full-text returns nothing for this. Trigram still catches both real Dhurandhar posts. The OR clause saves the reader.

  ---
  SQL parallel (so the structure stays clear):
  SELECT title,
         ts_rank(search_vector, plainto_tsquery('english', 'dhurandhar')) AS rank,
         word_similarity('dhurandhar', title) AS sim
  FROM posts_post
  WHERE (search_vector @@ plainto_tsquery('english', 'dhurandhar')
         AND ts_rank(search_vector, plainto_tsquery('english', 'dhurandhar')) >= 0.001)
     OR word_similarity('dhurandhar', title) >= 0.3
  ORDER BY rank DESC, sim DESC;

  The Django version is dramatically tidier than the raw SQL. That's the value of Q — keeps complex boolean filters readable.

