  But trigram is the real safety net. If a post had ONLY the typo'd spelling anywhere, full-text would miss it and trigram would still catch   
  it. Your DB just happened to have the correct spelling in the body, masking how brittle full-text alone would be.                            
                  
  ---
  Beat 5 — wire combined search into the API

  This is the final step for concept #9. Replace your current get_queryset in posts/api_views.py with this:

  from django.db.models import Q
  from django.contrib.postgres.search import (
      SearchQuery, SearchRank, TrigramWordSimilarity,
  )

  class PostViewSet(viewsets.ModelViewSet):
      serializer_class = PostSerializer
      permission_classes = [IsAuthenticatedOrReadOnly, IsAuthorOrReadOnly]

      def get_queryset(self):
          queryset = Post.objects.filter(published=True)

          search = self.request.query_params.get('search')
          if search:
              search_query = SearchQuery(search)
              queryset = (
                  queryset
                  .annotate(
                      rank=SearchRank('search_vector', search_query),
                      similarity=TrigramWordSimilarity(search, 'title'),
                  )
                  .filter(Q(rank__gte=0.001) | Q(similarity__gte=0.3))
                  .order_by('-rank', '-similarity')
              )

          return queryset

      def perform_create(self, serializer):
          serializer.save(author=self.request.user)

  What changed from concept #8's version:
  - Added two imports: Q and TrigramWordSimilarity
  - The .annotate() now adds two scores (rank from FT, similarity from trigram)
  - .filter() uses Q(...) | Q(...) for OR — match if either side passes
  - .order_by('-rank', '-similarity') — full-text matches surface first; among ties, trigram score breaks them

